## Concepts Practiced

This module demonstrates:

- Null and alternative hypotheses
- One-sample z-tests
- Standard errors
- Z-statistics
- P-values
- Significance levels
- Rejection regions
- Type I errors
- Sampling variability
- Standard normal distributions
- Uniform p-value distributions under H0
- Monte Carlo simulation
- Statistical validation using repeated simulation

---

## What I Learned

This simulation helped demonstrate that hypothesis testing does not eliminate the possibility of making an incorrect decision.

Even when the null hypothesis is completely true, random sampling variability can occasionally produce an unusually extreme sample.

If that sample produces a p-value below the chosen significance level, the test rejects the null hypothesis and creates a Type I error.

The significance level controls the long-run probability of this type of error. For example, when α = 0.05, approximately 5% of tests reject a true null hypothesis.

The simulation also showed why p-values are useful. Under a true null hypothesis, p-values are uniformly distributed, so the proportion of p-values below alpha is approximately equal to alpha itself.

Overall, the experiment demonstrates the connection between sampling variability, z-statistics, p-values, significance levels, and false-positive decisions in hypothesis testing.